Feature Plan: dalogin datasource legacy connection removal

Objective
- Collapse `DBConnectionManager` to a single pooled path backed by the `application.properties`
  Agroal datasource, and delete the `DriverManager` fallback along with its hardcoded credentials.

Relationship to the roadmap
- Implements inventory item 2 ("DB connection pooling ... remove remaining raw `DriverManager` fallback")
  from `speckit.system.refactoring-mentions-roadmap.plan`.
- Replaces the first portion of the originally planned `speckit.system.dalogin-datasource-pooling.*` triad.
- Partially overlaps roadmap item 4 (hardcoded credentials) by removing the two credential literals
  currently present in `CustomServletContextListener`; full secret externalization stays in its own plan.

Role ownership
- Implementation: `@backend-dev`
- Credential and log review: `@security-specialist`
- Verification: `@testing-engineer`

Baseline (as-found)
- Pooled path is already active: `CustomServletContextListener` injects `DataSource` and, when present,
  builds `new DBConnectionManager(dataSource)`.
- The fallback branch is therefore effectively dead in normal Quarkus startup, but it still compiles,
  still carries credentials, and still masks misconfiguration.
- `SQLAccess` obtains the manager via `context.getAttribute("DBManager")`; that indirection is retained
  in this feature and addressed by the CDI injection feature.

Design decisions
- `DBConnectionManager` survives only as a thin, single-constructor class at this edit step. It and
  the `"DBManager"` `ServletContext` attribute are deleted later in the same combined pass
  (`speckit.system.dalogin-persistence-servlet-adaptation.plan`, phase 5); nothing here is
  compiled or shipped in the intermediate shape.
- Prefer fail-fast over silent fallback: a missing datasource is a configuration defect.
- Set `acquisition-timeout` explicitly so pool exhaustion is observable instead of hanging.
- Delete `com.dalogin.utils.test` outright rather than sanitizing it. It is unreferenced scratch code
  with a `main` method; it has no callers to preserve and no tests depend on it. Leaving it would
  keep a live `DriverManager` path and a second credential pair in the module.
- Narrow `getConnection()` to `throws SQLException`. `ClassNotFoundException` was an artifact of
  `Class.forName`; keeping it would force callers to handle an exception that can no longer occur.
- Log the configured catalog name rather than calling `conn.getCatalog()` per acquisition, and log at
  DEBUG. The current line fires on every one of the 21 data-access methods.

Target state
- `DBConnectionManager` retains a single `DataSource`-based constructor.
- `getConnection()` delegates directly to `dataSource.getConnection()` and declares only
  `throws SQLException`.
- `CustomServletContextListener` has no conditional fallback branch.
- No credentials, no `DB_URL` default, and no JDBC driver class loading in source.
- `com.dalogin.utils.test` is gone.

Intent
- This is a forward-only structural cleanup. There is no rollback plan and no revert path is designed
  for; defects found after the change are fixed forward.

Implementation phases
1. Configuration
   - Uncomment and set `quarkus.datasource.jdbc.acquisition-timeout`.
   - Confirm `min-size` / `max-size` remain configuration-driven.
2. Connection manager simplification
   - Delete the `DriverManager` branch and the `Class.forName` call.
   - make it runnable in localhost and with minikube as well - check k8infra for deployment info.
   - Narrow the signature to `throws SQLException` and drop the now-impossible checked exception.
   - Make a null datasource an explicit startup/acquisition failure, when db is not running.
   - Remove closeConnection(), as quarkus take control.
3. Listener cleanup
   - Remove the `if (dataSource != null) ... else ...` branch.
   - Remove the hardcoded credentials and `DB_URL` fallback.
   - Remove the `contextDestroyed` call to `closeConnection()` if the method is dropped.
4. Dead code deletion
   - Delete `src/main/java/com/dalogin/utils/test.java`.
   - Confirm nothing referenced it. `SystemConstants.DB_CATALOG` loses its last readers
     (`utils/test`, `DBConnectionManager`) inside the combined pass, so delete it and its `DB_URL`
     read there; keep `getServiceUrl()`.
   - Remove the dead dalogin `DB_URL` env entries from `k8infra/quarkus-backend.yaml` and
     `k8infra/kubernetes.yaml`; the `QUARKUS_DATASOURCE_*` entries already carry minikube config.
5. Logging review
   - Reduce the acquisition log to DEBUG and report the configured catalog name, not `getCatalog()`.
   - Ensure connection logs do not print credentials or the full JDBC URL.

Validation strategy
- Do not compile this component independently. Continue through CDI injection, family splitting, and
  Servlet adaptation under `speckit.system.dalogin-persistence-servlet-adaptation.plan`.
- `mbook` and `mbooks` are not required; the test targets `dalogin-quarkus` directly.
- Registration, voucher, password-reset, and the bounded-pool check (`max-size=1` with a short
  acquisition timeout) are verified manually by the repository owner and do not block the change.

Risks and mitigations
- Risk: some deployment relied on the `DB_URL` environment fallback.
  - Mitigation: confirm `k8infra/quarkus-backend.yaml` supplies datasource configuration before merging.
- Risk: fail-fast converts a previously silent misconfiguration into a startup error.
  - Mitigation: this is the intended behavior; ensure the error message names the missing datasource.
- Risk: narrowing `getConnection()` breaks a caller that catches `ClassNotFoundException`.
  - Mitigation: no caller does. `SQLAccess` methods declare `throws Exception` and catch only
    `SQLException`; the compiler confirms this at the single build.
- Risk: deleting `utils/test` and `DBConnectionManager` removes every reader of
  `SystemConstants.DB_CATALOG`.
  - Mitigation: delete `DB_CATALOG`/`extractCatalog()` in the same pass; the compiler confirms no
    reader remains.
- Risk: connection leak becomes more visible once the pool is bounded.
  - Mitigation: verify every `SQLAccess` method uses try-with-resources; a leak found here is a real
    defect to fix forward.
